  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Countering Trafficking in Persons and Ensuring Assistance to Vulnerable Groups in Ukraine (CTIP/V)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The full-scale invasion of Ukraine by the Russian Federation in 2022 has exponentially increased the population&apos;s vulnerabilities to human trafficking and gender based violence (GBV), protection risks for children, international migrants and internally-displaced persons (IDPs), which root causes and drivers were already exacerbated by the significant economic stagnation in the country derived from the COVID-19 pandemic and the protracted armed conflict in eastern Ukraine. With this project, IOM aims to contribute to reducing trafficking in persons trends in Ukraine and sustain the national counter-trafficking response. This objective will be achieved through enabling fully-functioning state-led counter-trafficking response at national and local levels; implementing awareness raising activities and promoting strategic partnerships; and ensuring access to comprehensive reintegration assistance for victims of trafficking (VoTs), victims of exploitation, GBV survivors and other vulnerable groups, including by enhancing economic opportunities.</narrative>
  </description>
